    "sourcing": {
      "type": "kinsoul-materials-sourcing",
      "blocks": {
        "kerman": {
          "type": "region",
          "settings": {
            "region": "Kerman Province, Iran",
            "material": "Persian red & banded agate",
            "description": "<p>Kerman province in southeastern Iran is a professional-grade agate source: saturated orange-reds, deep banded browns, larger workable rough, stricter color grading at source. The hand-cut stones we use in Ember run 17–20 mm, with a translucency that changes as you turn the stone in the light.</p><p>Persian red agate runs roughly 2.5× the cost of Indian red agate. We chose it because the stone carries the bracelet.</p>",
            "products_label": "In these bracelets",
            "product_1_url": "/products/persian-red-agate-pearl-sterling-silver-bracelet",
            "product_1_name": "Ember — Persian red agate",
            "product_2_url": "/products/moqiao-black-agate-rutilated-quartz-pearl-bracelet",
            "product_2_name": "Terra — Persian banded agate",
            "product_3_url": "/products/aura-balance-bracelet-baroque-pearl-crystal-mixed-gemstones",
            "product_3_name": "Aura — Persian red agate (in palette)"
          }
        },
        "zhuji": {
          "type": "region",
          "settings": {
            "region": "Zhuji, China",
            "material": "Freshwater pearls",
            "description": "<p>Zhuji, in Zhejiang province, is the world's largest freshwater pearl cultivation region. The pearls are real — grown inside live freshwater mussels that produce nacre in thick, luminous layers. Zhuji pearls skew toward baroque (over 90% of freshwater pearls are baroque) because the mussel's mantle-tissue nucleation doesn't enforce roundness.</p><p>We use Zhuji pearls in four of the six bracelets, in threaded, bar, rice, and round forms.</p>",
            "products_label": "In these bracelets",
            "product_1_url": "/products/persian-red-agate-pearl-sterling-silver-bracelet",
            "product_1_name": "Ember — pearl bars",
            "product_2_url": "/products/freeform-amethyst-bracelet-with-freshwater-pearls",
            "product_2_name": "Serenity — threaded pearls",
            "product_3_url": "/products/zining-brazilian-yellow-quartz-clear-quartz-amethyst-bracelet",
            "product_3_name": "Soleil — round pearls"
          }
        },
        "australia": {
          "type": "region",
          "settings": {
            "region": "Australian waters",
            "material": "Baroque saltwater pearls",
            "description": "<p>The large baroque pearl that anchors Aura and Terra is a saltwater pearl from Australia. Saltwater baroques run about 1.5× the cost of freshwater baroques — they grow around a bead nucleus that shifts during development, which gives each pearl a singular, one-of-one shape that no amount of sorting can repeat.</p><p>Every baroque pearl we set is hand-picked for nacre integrity before it goes on a bracelet.</p>",
            "products_label": "In these bracelets",
            "product_1_url": "/products/aura-balance-bracelet-baroque-pearl-crystal-mixed-gemstones",
            "product_1_name": "Aura — baroque anchor pearl",
            "product_2_url": "/products/moqiao-black-agate-rutilated-quartz-pearl-bracelet",
            "product_2_name": "Terra — baroque saltwater"
          }
        },
        "bolivia": {
          "type": "region",
          "settings": {
            "region": "Bolivian Andes",
            "material": "Amethyst",
            "description": "<p>The deep-violet reputation of Bolivian amethyst comes from the quartz-rich rock formations high in the Andes. Colors run from soft lavender at one stone to deep violet at the next — a spectrum you see most clearly in Serenity's freeform cut, where the natural variation is preserved instead of polished out.</p><p>Bolivian amethyst appears in three of the six bracelets, as freeform stones in Serenity, rounds in Soleil, and in Aura's 12-stone palette.</p>",
            "products_label": "In these bracelets",
            "product_1_url": "/products/freeform-amethyst-bracelet-with-freshwater-pearls",
            "product_1_name": "Serenity — freeform amethyst",
            "product_2_url": "/products/zining-brazilian-yellow-quartz-clear-quartz-amethyst-bracelet",
            "product_2_name": "Soleil — amethyst rounds",
            "product_3_url": "/products/aura-balance-bracelet-baroque-pearl-crystal-mixed-gemstones",
            "product_3_name": "Aura — amethyst (in palette)"
          }
        },
        "uruguay": {
          "type": "region",
          "settings": {
            "region": "Uruguay",
            "material": "Natural citrine",
            "description": "<p>Natural unheated citrine is rare. Most commercial &ldquo;Brazilian citrine&rdquo; on the market is heat-treated amethyst from Brazilian mines. Uruguay holds the clearest reputation for naturally golden citrine — pale honey to deep amber, every stone a slightly different shade of sunlight because the color comes from trace iron inside the crystal itself.</p><p>Soleil uses Uruguayan natural citrine at the center, not the trade-name substitute.</p>",
            "products_label": "In these bracelets",
            "product_1_url": "/products/zining-brazilian-yellow-quartz-clear-quartz-amethyst-bracelet",
            "product_1_name": "Soleil — Uruguayan citrine",
            "product_2_url": "/products/aura-balance-bracelet-baroque-pearl-crystal-mixed-gemstones",
            "product_2_name": "Aura — citrine (in palette)"
          }
        },
        "brazil": {
          "type": "region",
          "settings": {
            "region": "Brazil",
            "material": "Tourmalinated quartz",
            "description": "<p>Tourmalinated quartz is clear crystal threaded with fine needle-like inclusions of black tourmaline — frozen, as if a brushstroke of dark ink were caught inside the stone. Brazil is the main source for this specific variety, and we use it in Obsidian paired with Mexican black agate.</p><p>It's often confused with rutilated quartz, which contains thinner, golden-copper rutile hairs; ours is tourmalinated — opaque black needles, not rutile.</p>",
            "products_label": "In these bracelets",
            "product_1_url": "/products/mozhu-persian-agate-baroque-pearl-bracelet",
            "product_1_name": "Obsidian — tourmalinated quartz",
            "product_2_url": "/products/aura-balance-bracelet-baroque-pearl-crystal-mixed-gemstones",
            "product_2_name": "Aura — tourmalinated quartz (in palette)"
          }
        },
        "mexico": {
          "type": "region",
          "settings": {
            "region": "Mexico",
            "material": "Black agate",
            "description": "<p>Obsidian's deep black agate comes from Mexico — dense, opaque, with faint translucent edges where the stone catches light. Cut from natural rough, each stone carries its own pattern of black, charcoal, and cream tones; no two Obsidians will have the same stone layout.</p><p>In many traditions, black agate is carried for inner resolve — strength that doesn't need to announce itself.</p>",
            "products_label": "In this bracelet",
            "product_1_url": "/products/mozhu-persian-agate-baroque-pearl-bracelet",
            "product_1_name": "Obsidian — Mexican black agate"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "kerman",
        "zhuji",
        "australia",
        "bolivia",
